35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The call-back trial reduced readmissions by a fifth, but the effect vanished when it stopped.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Toshio,,The booking system only logs named slots. Unstructured work disappears from reports entirely.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,The joint clinic failed because neither department owned the rota or the budget line.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"Therapy staff are leaving for city posts, but midwifery has unusual slack due to falling births.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The budget is down a quarter, and home visits earn no tariff. We must be precise.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Toshio, if work isn't logged, how do we prove value?","{""words"":10,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, we can't. That is the problem. The system is blind to ad-hoc care.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Servas, the data agreements take fourteen months. Any external link is a bottleneck.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Benjamin, that explains the fragility. But the midwifery slack is a real resource we have.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@All, if home visits are unfunded, we cannot build a program on them without a subsidy.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"The readmission data suggests home follow-up works, but only if sustained.","{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"The car park fills by eleven. A day unit would need dedicated space, which we lack.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,Chronic care coordination requires external data links. That is a fourteen-month delay.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Yumi,,Perinatal support fits the midwifery slack. It is a low-risk use of existing capacity.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,Perinatal support is on-site. It bills at the standard rate. It is financially viable.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,I am leaning toward Perinatal Support. It uses existing slack and is billable.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I agree. It fits within current room and booking constraints better than a day unit.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,I object to Chronic Care Coordination. The data agreement delay is a dealbreaker.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Yumi,,I support Perinatal Support. It leverages the midwifery capacity we currently have.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,I concur. It is the only option that is both clinically sound and financially self-sustaining.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
